<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barfleur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barfleur is a small port on the north-east coast of the Cotentin peninsula in Normandy. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was one of the main harbours for crossings between Normandy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In 1120 the White Ship sank off Barfleur, drowning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-adelin">William Adelin</a>, heir of Henry I of England, which led to the succession war known as the Anarchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barfleur.jpg" alt="Barfleur" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JackyM59 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_port_de_Barfleur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
